export const STAM_1929_COMPLETE_ETHNOGRAPHIC_SUMMARY = {
  "sourceId": "stam-bahanga-1929",
  "citation": "Stam, Rev. N. (Mill Hill Society, Mumias, Kavirondo, East Africa) (1929). \"The Bahanga.\" Publications of the Catholic Anthropological Conference, Vol. I, No. 4, pp. 143–179, August, 1929, Washington, D.C., U.S.A. (with 1 Map and 2 Text Figures).",
  "sections": {
    "frontMatterAndMap": "Cover & Series Listing (pp. 143, 145): Publications of the Catholic Anthropological Conference, Vol. I, No. 4 (August, 1929, price $.60; alongside Vol. I No. 1 Joseph Meier's Adoption Among the Gunantuna pp. 1–98, No. 2 Stephen Richarz's The Age of the Human Race in the Light of Geology pp. 99–115, No. 3 Francis Lambrecht's Ifugaw Villages and Houses pp. 117–141, and the quarterly bulletin Primitive Man). Map on p. 145 ('Territory of the Bahanga'): Country = Buhanga; Language = Luhanga; Native (sing.) = Muhanga; Natives (plur.) = Bahanga; showing the Nzoia R., Lusumu R., Lugose R., Viratsi R., Mumias, Matawa, Imanga, Matungo, Khyimuli, Khyira, Ndangalasya, Lwanda, Babukusu, Bakhayo, Kabras, Nandi, Batsotso, Bamarama, Nilotic Kavirondo, and Banyala.",
    "tribalHistoryAndGenealogy": "Tribal History & Complete Royal Genealogy (pp. 143–148): Ancestor Kwandedi (born in Teriki country, settled at Khyira in Kabras near the Nandi border) had two sons, Wanga and Khabiagala, who settled at Bwagwe (Kabras) and quarreled until Wanga left secretly at night for Imanga hill south of the Lusumu river, serving petty Maragoli chief Muyima while feigning a lame arm under his skin cloak to hide his royal thick copper bracelet until a woman who boiled his food spied him washing his hands after dark. Joined at Bijifi by three devoted Kabras clans (Bakyero/Bakhyero, Banamagwa, and Bakhyikawa), Wanga crossed the Lusumu river, drove out the Bamakambo (who turned on the Maragoli in Marama), built his stronghold at Mumias, and crossed the Nzoya river to build a second fort on Matungo hill, where he died and was buried in his hut. Succeeded by Waballa (who sawed the sharp tips off unmanageable long-horned cattle in Bukhayo, where he was murdered through the jealousy of his brother Murono). Dying Waballa sent Wakhalivu (head of the Bakyero kingmaking clan) with the copper bracelet and four arrows to Matawa (4 miles from Mumias), instructing that his newborn son be named Muswi ('a name of a girl who has not brought forth'); as a boy Muswi slew four of Murono's attackers with the four arrows and drove Murono across the Nzoya to Marakalu near Ndangalasya (where Murono's son Kitai robbed his people until they deserted to Muswi). Muswi was succeeded peacefully by Wamukoya [I], whose sons Netya and Kweyu fought for the chieftainship: Kweyu retired to Khyimuli (NE of Mumias, attacked by the Abekwe and Abarebe; father of Lumbasi → Sakwa → Lutomiya [father of Rapando, present chief of Mukulu], Lwambami, & Kiwundu), while Netya fought the Abetakho (Kakamega), Babukusu (Kitosh), Wamia, and Nilotic Kavirondo, slew Uasin-Gishu Masai guests at a feast, and was besieged at Mumias and killed at Lwanda. At the Masai's request Netya's son Osundwa [I] ('death or victory') became chief, followed by Wamukoya [II] (famine and raids), Khyihundu (who paid cattle tribute to the Masai until a rifle-armed Swahili caravan helped defeat surrounding tribes), and Mumia (of the royal Bakhitsetse family; ~20 when Thomson passed through; proclaimed paramount chief by the British East Africa Company next to its Sudanese garrison, with brothers Mulama in Marama, Katima in Samia, Murunga in North Kitosh & Wamia, and Were in Wahola; two principal kraals in Mumias and Jinja, ~70 children mostly Catholic, disliked Dr. Carl Peters, and retired in 1928 in favor of Osundwa [II], son of his brother Kiteki, while remaining mwami till death).",
    "burialOfChiefAndCommoners": "Death & Burial of a Chief (pp. 148–149) and Commoners (pp. 149–152, Fig. 1 p. 150): A dying chief of the Bakhitsetse family is strangled by the head and elders of the Bakhyero clan and buried naked in a sitting posture sewn inside a fresh bull's hide in the middle of his hut with a beer-sucker in his mouth, two pieces of reedgrass under the eyelids to keep the eyes open, and a bottomless beerpot over his head above ground tended solely by his first wife until the head decays; 1–2 years later his successor holds 'to fix the grave of the chief' (okhusenera ekhyirindwa ekhyo mwami), spears a bull, washes the exhumed bones with water, polishes them with butter, places them in a beerpot, and carries them in procession to Matungo under the sacred trees. For commoners (death = okhufwa, burial = okhuyaberwa, grave = ekhyirindwa, Fig. 1 p. 150): kraal owner is buried naked inside the middle of his hut on his right side (mourners shave right forehead); woman outside to the left of the entrance on her left side (shave left); male child outside under the right eaves; female child outside under the left eaves (shave middle forehead); wild fig bark-cloth leaves are placed under the right ear and (with a pierced leaf) over the left ear so the dead can hear sacrifices; followed by the burial chicken (inkokho eyo mukulikho), great bewailing (okhuba khu maziga), 'to return the bones' (okhukalusya evigumba, without greeting anyone or looking back on the road), 'to fasten the grave' (okhusenera ekhyirindwa) & burial dance (omuyima) 1 year later, and river-bank burning of undecomposed corpses causing nightmares.",
    "sacrificesSacredStonesAndSpecialRites": "Sacrifices, Sacred Stones (Fig. 2 p. 153) & Five Special Sacrifices (pp. 152–158): Officiated by the sacrificer (omumali owe misango; sacrifice = omusango; to sacrifice = okhumala omusango). Fig. 2 (p. 153) Kraal Layout: (1) two ancestral stones (amagina ake misambwa: right stone 1 ft × 1.5 ft inherited by eldest son, left stone 4–5 in. inherited by second son) placed 10 ft in front of the first wife's hut flanking a 4-ft lusambwa / lusiola pole (Markhamia platycalyx, from the khyemebekho / khyembekho sacred grove; from obsolete samba 'to build/erect' → reversive sambula 'to break down' by changing -a to -ula); (2) miniature sacrificial hut (khatsu khe misango) to the left of the first wife's hut (used with a female quail isindu eye misango at sowing); and (3) two flat 'stones on the pathway' (amagina ako lunganyi) outside the kraal entrance dedicated to God (Nasayi / Were). Includes the 3-generation invocation ('abakuku mulire omu; evinambwa mulire omu; evisoni mulire omu', p. 155), sacrificial beer/cow/chicken/quail (amalwa / ingombe / inkokho / isindu eye misango) & earless sacred bull (p. 156), 4 cases of okhutisa imbusi (wife with skull fracture, beaten wife running naked into a hut, broken waterpot past a hut, and dripping grass-ash salt filter while gossiping, pp. 156–158), and the white naming fowl held against the center pole with 2 wing feathers stuck in the ground to name a crying 2-day baby after a parent-in-law (inkokho eyakulikhirwamwo, p. 158).",
    "rainmakingReligionPractitionersAndOmens": "Sacred Fig Tree, Rainmaking, Religion, Practitioners, Ordeals & 10 Omens (pp. 158–170): Rainmaking at the sacred wild fig tree (Artocarpus, khyembekho) where a boy blows through a hollow reed into a boiling pot with milky charm powder until soap-suds froth overflows (and Mumia's plea to Fr. Stam: 'Beseech God because my endeavors have been in vain', p. 159); Nasayi ('the one adored' < sayo) & Were, morning spitting towards the sun (liyuba), 'Were ankhonyere' ('God has helped me', p. 160), 'Nasayi akhulinde' ('May God protect you', p. 161), self-digging hoes & the Chameleon-in-the-beerpot vs. Snake origin-of-death myth (p. 161), immortal soul/shadow (ekhyinekha) & evil spirits (vikhyeno, p. 162); Masai-grave spirit possession (omusebe / musebe / sitani, pp. 162–163); bewitchers (balosi, sg. mulosi, pp. 163–164); herbalist/cupping surgeon (omundu owe misala, pp. 164–165); charm doctor (omundu owe mikalo, with banana-bark cattle-plague bundle and hollow reed / wild pig's tooth court ash charm, pp. 165–166); diviner (omufumo, pp. 166–167); poison-man (omufira, with poison-tipped walking stick & wild onion, 2-cow cure fee, p. 167); judicial ordeals (ekhyilulu: pounded-stick draught with 2-cow theft / 3-cow false accusation fine, and Skull Ordeal, pp. 167–168); and all 10 Omens (amayoni amabi, 'bad birds', pp. 169–170).",
    "adoptionTwinsMarriageTotemsAndCircumcision": "Foundling Adoption (Makokha), Twins (Amakhwana), Marriage, Totems (Musiro), Mutilations & Circumcision (pp. 170–179): Roadway foundling adopted with loin-fringe, mussel shell, bead, and ear ring as Makokha ('rubbish', p. 171); 2-month twin confinement (amakhwana) & okhutsiya khu makwana purification across a 10-mile radius with Passifloraceae creeper, Trifolium clover, Caesalpiniaceae plant, and sheep dung (pp. 172–173); marriage (okhutekherana 'to boil for one another'), dowry (okhukhua omukhana: 1 young bull + 1 heifer + 8 cows, 3 goats = 1 cow), bride's live quail beak & spear (10s + 4–5s + 4s presents), jumping over the goat (okhusira imbusi), virginity cow (ingombe yarando), and 8-piece cooking outfit after 5–6 months with water-throwing and brother's stick-beating rite earning a heifer (pp. 173–176); patrilineal non-exogamous totems (musiro: all Bahanga abstain from Isaac's antelope Boöcercus eurycerus, Marama Bahanga from eland; women abstain from chickens, guinea fowl, eggs, sheep, suckling goat & lepidosiren mudfish, pp. 176–177); extraction of 4 lower incisors at age ~10, 2 rows of ~25 forehead incisions, and 3-stroke cheek mark (\\|/, 1.25 in.) burnt with isambirire grass, saliva, and cobwebs (p. 178); and standing circumcision (okhukhyebwa) every 2–3 years at ages 15–25 by Kakalelwa (Abarebe) operators followed by 4–5 months in irumbi lodges (pp. 178–179)."
  }
} as const;

export const LINDBLOM_1920_COMPLETE_ETHNOGRAPHIC_SUMMARY = {
  "sourceId": "lindblom-akamba-1920",
  "citation": "Lindblom, Gerhard (1920). The Akamba in British East Africa: An Ethnological Monograph. 2nd Edition, Enlarged. Archives d'Études Orientales, publiées par J.-A. Lundell, Vol. 17. Uppsala: Appelbergs Boktryckeri Aktiebolag, 1918–1920, pp. I–XII, 1–607, Figs. 1–167.",
  "sections": {
    "frontMatterIntroductionAndChapter1": "Front Matter, Sponsors, Dedication & Introduction (pp. I–XII, 1–7): Published at the expense of Forges et Aciéries d'Avesta (M. Axel Ax:son Johnson), Phil. Dr. Frans Kempe (Hernösand-Stockholm), A. B. Svenska Kullager-Fabriken (SKF, Gotembourg), Fabrique de Cuir de L. A. Matton (Gefle), and Soc. anon. Nordstjernan (Johnson Lignes, M. Axel Ax:son Johnson); 1st ed. publicly defended as an Inaugural Dissertation at Upsala on May 27th, 1916 (Chs. I–XII = Chs. I–V, VII–XI, XIII of 2nd ed.); 2nd ed. enlarged (1918–1920) dedicated 'TO COUNT ERIC VON ROSEN IN GRATEFUL AFFECTION'; based on fieldwork in East Africa (Dec. 1910 – June 1912, among the Akamba Jan.–Nov. 1911 and Jan.–Mar. 1912); native custom formula 'ni matindu ma andu ala ma tænə' ('It is a custom handed down from our fathers', p. 3); reviews 18 prior works (Krapf 1858/1860, Hildebrandt 1878, Kolb 1898, Säuberlich 1899, Decle 1900, Hofmann 1901, Brutzer 1902/1905/1910, Hobley 1910/1911/1912, Ch. Dundas 1913/1915, Höhnel 1892, Gregory 1896, H. B. Johnstone 1902, H. R. Tate 1904) and Lindblom's own Notes on the Kamba Language (Archives d'Études Orientales Vol. 10, Upsala 1919); names 7 primary Kamba informants (p. 6): 1. Kioko wa Malata (Machakos), 2. Malata wa Kiambi (specialist in ŋgondu & cow twin-birth rites, p. 489), 3. Mbonge wa Kithome (ītīma herbal doctor, Machakos, p. 319), 4. Muhindi (elder, Machakos), 5. Mbindia / Vindia (mundu muə at Kibwezi who initiated Lindblom into the 3rd nžaīko [mbæβanī] and performed conjuring tricks kīama, pp. 61, 266–268), 6. Makiti (headman at Kibwezi, þaβu case pp. 299–300), and 7. Muli (near Ikutha, Atharaka kīþītū rock crevice, pp. 170–171). Ch. I Country, Divisions, Neighbours & Ikutha Rainfall Table (pp. 9–26 & Addenda pp. 569–574): Triangle ~225 km N–S × 130 km E–W (~230,000 souls in Ukamba proper) from Donyo Sabuk (Kamba: kīma kīa nžaβī 'the nžaβī bean mountain') to Mumoni, Mtoto Andei (from Kamba mutītu wa ndeī 'the vulture forest'), and Mt. Noka (hunting Anoka speaking a Kamba dialect, p. 10 fn. 1), plus diaspora colonies in Rabai (emigrated in the 1836 famine īoa īa kudīkīla mbua; nicknamed atumwa 'slaves' or madīkīlambua 'rain-followers', p. 11), Usagara (Mamboia), Upare, Usambara, Uguru (Kerenge, Gedja), Bagamoyo (Windi/Saadani wars with Wadoe), Kidete, and Usaramo (Mkamba); 4 divisions & dialects: (1) Ulu/Yulu (speaking kīkamba kīluŋgalu 'real Kikamba', with SW Kīluŋgu who call other Ulu Akamba æβau 'cousins'), (2) East Ukamba/Kitui (called aþaisu speaking kīþaisu; cattle-rich ŋgoβə vs. beehive-climbing īkulī 'baboons', p. 16), (3) Kīkumbuliu, and (4) Mumonī; Kamba ethnonyms for neighbours (akīkuyu, akaβī / akīþoŋgo / alakī [Masai], andī [Wataita], aþaka [Atharaka], asumba / maþomba [Swahili], aswī [Wagiriama, lang. kīswī, country uswīnī], aluluma [Waduruma], ambele [Wambere], atwa / akala [Galla], aŋgoloŋgo [Somali], anoβī [Nubians], asuŋgu / andu atunə [Europeans]; Swahili name for Akamba: Waumanguo / Warimangao; Masai name: el lunghu; Coast Akamba name for Ulu Akamba: nžaβī); and Missionary J. Hofmann's 11-year Ikutha monthly rainfall table in German zoll (p. 24: 1897=17.36, 1899=9.22 [Great Famine īoa īa ŋgalī / muβuŋga], 1902=29.74, 1903=21.75, 1904=32.15, 1905=24.57, 1906=38.94, 1907=26.34, 1908=16.71, 1909=21.26, 1910=20.49).",
    "part1IndividualLifeChapters2To7": "Part I Individual Life (Chs. II–VII, pp. 27–110, Figs. 1–16): Ch. II Child-Birth & Menstruation (pp. 29–41): pregnancy ('naβītanītie mwæī'), navel cord (mukautī), placenta (nsuu / ŋgua īa kana), birth charm (nðesīo), premature child (muīndə), wild fig (kīumo) & īkoka grass water rite, even-numbered women's feast eating utīndītī (īsīo) & ŋgīma, 4th-day iron neck-chain (īþa, before which infant is kīīmu), turning the child (olula kana: 'nīnīonīə nðakamə nīnaolulə kana', p. 34), breech birth (mundu wa kīu / mumo), caul (ŋgunīkīlīa), cutting upper teeth first (mundu wa kīumīlo / kīludu), twins (maβaþa), abortion plants (mwae soot, kīlīa mbitī, mutanda-mbo, ma-mbumbu), menstruation (mwæŋgə) & kuseuβīa mwītū. Ch. III Circumcision & The 3 Nžaīko Initiations (pp. 42–71, Figs. 1–16): (1) nžaīko īla nīnī / nžaīko īa kaβīu / nžaīko īekondə (operator mwaīkī, foreskin īkondə / īkolə); (2) nžaīko īla nænə / nžaīko īa mbusīa ('of the rhinoceros') / nžaīko īa mbaβī (7 days in þano: hut īβunu / kīeŋgo kīa nžaīko / īlwanda, novices asīŋgī, guardians aβwīkī [duty kuta], song-leader ŋguī, obstacle songs madīŋgo / mukīamo & alternating kīno / kea songs, roaring mbusīa pot blown with mwaeþa tube ['kwaþa mbusīa', 'wīkou nī mwana wa ŋganīa'], maŋgī bird-arrows, 80-cm musaī [ukaī] pictographic sticks of muþīwa wood [Figs. 1–8: þomə yard, tortoise, mumbə stool seat, python, cow's tail, stars, millipede/beehive/moon] & sand drawings [Figs. 9–16: snake, sun with ray legs, 3-legged stool, moon & stars, chain, centipede, cowry belt, narrow-necked calabash], 5th-day wild fig mumbu prayer ['kīumbu kī, nītukīə ukuβoīa, utunæŋgə wumbu wa uīkīīa asīŋgī'] & glans cut muβwa, and 7th-day sham Masai cattle raid pelting ndoŋgu fruits & nžuma dances); (3) nžaīko īa aumə ('circumcision of the men', called mbæβanī in Kikumbuliu / mbaβanī in Kitui & Mumoni, pp. 60–68: 10-cow fine or death for revealing secrets, V-angled mbæβanī pipes of kīusīa wood bound with mwaþa fibre & smeared with muīo, 4 rank fires [asīŋgī, ŋgala, aβwīkī, nžama], ordeal tests by the ŋgala [sucking sand, swallowing excrement crying 'aah!', pulling peg with teeth squatting handless, gauntlet, wood-tied erection & water-pit copulation], 4 Kikumbuliu secret twig/path recognition signs p. 67, forehead shaving 'nītænzīə asīŋgī' & rebirth kusīawa). Ch. IV Marriage (kwasīa / kuþoa mwītū, pp. 72–88): proposal goats (mbuī sīa nðeo), beer (kuβīkīla uki waβīkīla mbuī & wa uþambīa nžælə), slaughter buck (ndæŋgə īa kwītīa mbuī nðakamə nðī), bride-price (2 cows + 2 bulls/oxen + 40–50 goats; in Kikumbuliu 60–100 goats; cattle called kīīŋgo 'pot splinter'), kuþoka food gifts (child named nðokī), weeping songs (mbaþī sīa maīo), mother-in-law goat (mbuī īa kwona kana), barren woman (ŋguŋguu), impotent man (ndæwa), tally stick (kīka kīa kutala mbuī), wife-lending (kudīta), sole brother (kīmwæmwə), chief wife (kīβætī kīnænə / kīku), widow (muka wa ndīwa), and 26-family Machakos demographic table (p. 87: 26 fathers, 52 wives, 100 living boys, 70 living girls, 61 dead children). Ch. V Nðonī Avoidance, 11 Kwiūa Name-Taboo Pairs & Women's Language (pp. 89–98 & Addenda p. 574): muþonī (greeting 'muþonwa!'), buying fire (þoa mwakī), Kisese's 'killing nðonī' (ua nðonī) & brother-in-law ua ŋgeano; 11 kwiūa pairs (pp. 93–94: kīoko→unī, kīlonžo→ŋguaīa, īlondu→ŋgondu, nžukī→ndoŋgoŋgī, nžoka→niamu īa ndī / īkūū, wa mbua→nduþu, ŋgomo→ndæwa, mbesa→īβulī, -æu/nžaū→ndeuβu, þomə→īβuβeonī / muumaloni, mwatū→mwaŋgo, ītwīku→wīīuka, mwæī→musesīa nðī); plus 7 women's words (p. 574: mbunu, kīβwū, kīseβə, kusonolīa, īŋa / īsuma mīīo, mbuþæŋgə, ukatī). Ch. VI Complete Kinship Terminology (pp. 99–102): naū/tata/asa (au, īþə), mwaitū/inīa/nīa/nana/mukwə (mwænīu), mwanaaīa vs. mwanaasa/mwanaæþə, mwītūīaīa vs. mwītūī-asa, mukuwa (īkīþoβī vs. īlumaita), mwīnawa, mwīmæwa, muka/kīβætī/kīwandu, umau, susu/usū, nžukulu/musukua, mwændwasa, mama/mwīβau/īnaīmu/īnaumə, mwændīa, sīīawa, mwamwa, ukulu, ŋga- & sīo-. Ch. VII Death & Burial (pp. 103–110): flexed burial on right side for man ('kwoko kwa aumə') and left side for woman ('kwoko kwa aka'), grandson turns first sod (βululīlīa), deserted hut (mbea), knocking out 2 lower incisors of dead young child, purification by mutumīa wa makwa / uku with goat chyme (muīo from kīþatīa / kīþīlīko) & ŋgondu, kuseuβīa kīβætī, and proxy corpse-stick (p. 109).",
    "part2SociologyChapters8To12": "Part II Sociology (Chs. VIII–XII, pp. 111–205, Figs. 17–54): Ch. VIII Clan System (Mbaī), Totems (Niamu), Brands (Figs. 17–51) & Blood-Brotherhood (pp. 113–142): prefix mba-; totem question 'niamu īaku nī īaū?'; 23 Machakos clans & sub-clans (pp. 136–137: 1. aiīenī, 2. anžaunī [jackal mbīwa; unlucky brides], 3. anžīu [drongo kīndalī; evil eye kīænī neutralized by spitting], 4. awinī [hyena mbitī], 5. æombə [mba-maī & mba-mululu; monkey ŋgīma], 6. akīþumbə / akīmiī [bushbuck nðwaīa; women forbidden to make pottery], 7. aþaŋga / æþaŋga [iron sand kīlea], 8. ætaŋgwa [mba-mulela/ŋguŋguu, mba-katætī, mba-muþækə, mba-mukuβa; baboon ŋgulī/īlaī & hawk mbulusīa], 9. asī & 10. amuī [lion munīambu; never eat or touch liver ītema; heal burns by chewing musu leaves fasting], 11. akītutu [hawk mbulusīa], 12. atwī [smiths], 13. amwīī / amwæī [moon mwæī; never sweep refuse out of hut on last day of month], 14. akītondo [mba-mbulī & mba-nðīmba; crow ŋguŋguu], 15. amumonī [wild fig mumo], 16. ambua [rain mbua], 17. akītuo [shoulder kītuo], 18. æwanī [leopard ŋgo], 19. amunda [mba-muīeþīa & mba-nžalu; leopard ŋgo], 20. amutī, 21. amutæī [secretary bird nðeī; 1-hole beehive mark], 22. aæī / mba-mulata-īβīa / mba-kīīmu / mba-anžīkwa [descended from first couple on Mt. Nzaui; forbidden to swing a child in one's arms lest it rise into the air and vanish], 23. anðunðu [porcupine nžæ & bat uβuβu]) plus Kikumbuliu (amwīlu, anžī), Ikutha (awīlī, mba-mule-ītema), Kitui (mba-kītuku, mba-ŋgo) & Mumoni (aŋgokī, kana/aæī [green parrot ŋgwæī], p. 138); cattle brands (kīo < oa, Figs. 17–22), arrow marks (uβano, Figs. 23–38), beehive marks (Figs. 39–51), and blood-brotherhood & breast-sucking asylum (pp. 140–142). Chs. IX–X Age/Rank Classes & Governance (pp. 143–151): kana → kaβīsī → kīβīsī → mwanakə → nðælə → mutumīa (stool mumbə) → atumīa ma nžama / atumīa ma īþæmbo (forked staff maka; fem. īβætī sīa īþæmbo); 5 Meat-Sharing Promotions (kukula / kukusa, pp. 144–145: 1. 1 goat = mutumīa wa mutwə [goat's head], 2. 1st bull = lower leg, 3. 2nd bull = upper leg, 4. 3rd bull = loins & brisket, 5. 4th bull = hump kīaə, 6. 5th bull = cattle tongue & head); kīsuka police club, age-grade īīka / ndukə, and Kitui leader Kivui (p. 149). Ch. XI Administration of Law & Customary Right (pp. 152–185, Fig. 52): lawsuit nžwanī ('nžwanī yanīwa manžī', judge sīla / sīlīla); homicide blood-money (man in Ulu = 11 cows + 1 bull, including 11th cow ŋgombə īa ndulota 'cow of the broken bow' to chief widow & 1st cow ŋgombə īa wumo paid by killer; in Kitui = 13 cows + 1 bull; woman in Ulu = 4–5 cows + 1 bull; in Kitui = 7 cows + 1 bull, pp. 154–156); injury fines & post-mortem autopsy (p. 157); honey-theft fine (1 bull + 5 goats, doubled/trebled, p. 160); boycotted outcast mukoə ('saliva', p. 161); land fence goat mbuī īa maþanzū (p. 164); kīþītū (muma, Fig. 52 p. 166) placed on 3 stones with mwæa twigs while swearer stands naked on 7 stones striking it with a mukulwa twig (kuna / kuīa kīþītū: 'mukancæŋga ŋgombə īmwə; nīkīsīoka kwænda īngī ītīna, ŋgaīwa nī kīu!', p. 169, carried with mutton-fat hands by musænzælī bast and purified with kīluŋgu sap) vs. relative oath ndundu (bull gall-bladder pierced with mwæa thorn, p. 172); 6 Ordeals (pp. 173–176: A. kīþītū kīa kīβīu red-hot knife, B. red-hot needle through lower lip, C. kīuma 2 china beads in eyes, D. ŋgunīko wax-plugged water calabash, E. mbæŋgolo bark poison, F. jumping over burning basket); kīŋgolə lynch-law (pp. 176–180, case of Kaβuβa); women's collective drum intervention (kīþæmbə, pp. 180–182); and 4 Curses (kīumo, pp. 182–185: father urinating on red-hot kīo branding iron toward setting sun [revoked by milk+Eleusine kīaþīmo], mother burning grain or splashing water from ndamī loin-cloth, and youths' dance-boycott curse striking a red china bead kīto against a girl with ŋgulu 'self-will'). Ch. XII Warfare (pp. 186–205, Figs. 53–54): leaders asīlīlī, spies aþīanī, soporific fire medicine (Masai ol umigumi = Kamba muβa, p. 205), 3 tactical divisions—(1) muena (50% anakə + 50% nðælə surrounding Masai warriors' kraal mbīlī), (2) ŋgīla (seizing cattle), (3) īta (driving booty away)—Thomson's gazelle horn war-flute ŋgulī (Fig. 53, blowing 'aka-βī, aka-βī, mæ-kuka, mæ-kuka!'), zebra-mane chest frame wea (Fig. 54), unburied fallen warriors, purifying captured weapons (matuso) by shooting a wooden arrow (tula mbaŋga 'break the peril'), slayer titles mutīætumo (spear), mutīota (bow), mutīoβīu (sword), mutīaŋgua (dress), mutīaþīaka (quiver), mutīando (scabbard), split ox-skin finger strip ukwalo (pl. ŋgwalə), kwaŋga victory procession & song (p. 199 fn. 1), women's 'līlī, līlī, līlī!' trills, and 'nītusæuβīə uta wa mwana' (p. 200).",
    "part3BeliefAndScienceChapters13To19": "Part III Belief and Science (Chs. XIII–XIX, pp. 207–354, Figs. 55–63): Ch. XIII Religion (pp. 209–253, Figs. 55–59): soul/shadow kīu vs. breath/life þaīu / mīæβa; ancestral spirits aīmu (sg. īīmu, dwelling underground or on Mt. Kyumbe, Kivauni, Muutha, Nengia, Nsambani & Kilui's Pond; 8 spirit tales pp. 225–228; elephant = spirits' cattle, tortoise = spirits' grindstone, praying mantis = mwaŋgī wa aīmu / muŋgaīmu 'spirit's snuff-box', spider's web = woman spirit's kīondo, p. 213); sacrifices (kīþaŋgona: 14 pieces of meat or 7+7 with guest, p. 218) at sacred fig/baobab groves (īþæmbo, Figs. 55–56, with atumīa ma īþæmbo, anakə ma īþæmbo / atumīa anīnī, and īβætī sīa īþæmbo initiated at the mboka banana festival); foreign spirits (mbæβo, kīsulīa cured with maβuo & 3 belly feathers, kīlumi exorcism dance [Figs. 57–58] with kīutu woodpecker-hole woodflour, and 1906–1908 kīesu pith-helmet mania); Supreme Creator Muluŋgu / Ŋgaī = Mumbī ('the Fashioner/Potter') & Mwatwaŋgagī ('the Cleaver', p. 244; calabash carving Fig. 59 p. 251; verbatim child-birth & rain prayers pp. 244–245; N. Söderblom's Producer theory p. 250); and 2 myths (Creation from termite hole muþumbīnī & Mt. Nsaue, and Chameleon vs. Weaver-Bird origin of death, pp. 252–253). Ch. XIV Medicine-Men, Magic & Purifications (pp. 254–310, Figs. 60–62): mundu muə (born with peg, 5 stones, or red mbuu seeds) vs. herbalist ītīma vs. sorcerer mundu mwoī (witchcraft woī; anti-witchcraft drink makīo sold by Mutune wa Taula of Nzaui, p. 283); divination (kuausīa, Fig. 60) with wire musical bow + kītītī gourd shaking mbu pebbles/seeds onto leopard skin; mutī (herb) vs. muþæa (magic medicine activated by ritual key kīþīao < þīaa, p. 270); epidemic expulsion (kwīndukīa uwau, pp. 271–275) through the unīka banana/post arch with musoka garlands, mutaa sprinkling, munaβu whip, and 7-day right-instep goat-skin strip (mwītano: 'mwænə mwīmu, osa nusu!'), or offerings on Mt. Mutitu to one-legged spirit Mwītualalī & rainbow-serpent mukuŋga-mbua (rainbow utaþī); women's ŋgolano rain march to Ngunu (p. 277); 9 amulets (kīþītū / mbīŋgu, pp. 285–288); name magic (kūtīea, pp. 288–290: elephants called nde ŋgu, mbonda malīa, wata, maβīa; hippo called ŋgwaūlə; rain called Ŋgaī; honey-jar called kīnaþī; carrion called maþaŋgo); omens (muþana musæo / muþuku: twitching kīoīo, sneezing kwaþīmwa → 'kula mwana wa ŋganīa!', and red-headed woodpecker ŋgomakomī with 6 directional calls: front mīsīa, behind ŋguŋguo, high right īna, low right βwaþī wa aumə, high left womu, low left βwaþī wa aka, p. 294); 17 ŋgondu purification substances (rock hyrax kīgond/kīkīla called īlondū when snared, mutaa, mulīndītī, kīoŋgwa, mukunda mbuī, mulalə, etc.) curing þaβu (-kuþunūa) & makwa (Kesungu of Kilungu taught by Siomuteti, pp. 295–303); snake-charmers Kimia of Ikutha & Kivuvu inoculating βīnīa ('power', Fig. 62 pp. 303–306); and magic of odd numbers (mwa, especially 7, vs. ritual 3, 4, 7, 14, pp. 306–310). Chs. XV–XVI Medicine, Surgery, Botany & Zoology (pp. 311–333): splints, uvula (kalīmə) excision with giraffe hairs, thorn-pinned wound suturing, 45+ medicinal plants (including severe kīaþī), 15+ magical/taboo fuel trees (muniī, mukau, kīβæ), and zoological lore (gerenuk kawīla īma, road-rat katīwa nī nžīa, hornbill īlumī, hammerkop ŋgunə, oxpecker ndīæī, house-snake sīomeluka / kīænda ndæto, giant snake īaīa, Solanum snake ŋguluku, blindworm kīþī, and hiding severed elephant trunk tip & female dugs from novices). Chs. XVII–XIX Cosmology, 9 Famines, 11-Month Calendars & History (pp. 334–354, Fig. 63): hail (maβīa), clouds (matu), fog (mumbī), rainbow (utaþī), lightning (utīsī), thunder (kītandalīkī / kītundumu), stars (ndata), evening star (ŋgæmandī), morning star (kithioi), comet (ndata īa kīsīþə / īoa), rock crystal (maβīa ma ndata), solar eclipse (sīua īaþotə muþæna), lunar halo (kīβuīo), male vs. female moon (ndamba vs. muka, Fig. 63), solar observatory (kīsūæsīo kīa sīua), cricket (ŋgīlī) & Crinum (īla mwaka); 9 historical famines (īoa, pp. 339–340: 1. malakwə 1908–09, 2. muβuŋga / ŋgalī 1898–99, 3. ndata ~1888, 4. ŋgætīsīə, 5. uβoə, 6. kīasa, 7. īoŋgela, 8. īta ~1850, 9. kudīkīla mbua ~1836); 3 lists of the 11-month calendar (pp. 341–342: mwa/kaīa, wīma, wīu/mwanža, mweu, wa katano/tulīa, wa þandatu/þanu wa mbēe, wa mwonža, wa nīanīa, wa kænda, wa īkumi, muβīu, oŋgonono/wīŋgonono); compass points (East (m)uīnīlo wa sīua, West uþuīlo / ndīwīlo wa sīua, horizon kītulīa kīa ītu); first-time traveler (muþo) sucking bow-tip before drinking river water (p. 347); and legend of the 3 brothers Mukaβī, Mukīkuyu & Mukamba (pp. 353–354).",
    "part4ArtDressMusicAndGamesChapters20To23": "Part IV Art, Dress, Ornaments, Music, Dance & Games (Chs. XX–XXIII, pp. 355–428, Figs. 64–120): Ch. XX Decorative Art (pp. 357–370, Figs. 64–76): carved/stippled calabashes (Figs. 64–71, including Mt. Kilimanjaro, hunting scene Fig. 68, sīo-manðala crab Fig. 69b), painted drums (Figs. 72–73), wire-bound wooden youth statue & staff (Figs. 74–75), and wire-inlaid elder's stool (mumbə, Fig. 76). Ch. XXI Clothing, Ornaments, Hair, Perfume, Cicatrization & Teeth-Chipping (pp. 371–397, Figs. 77–108): skin dress (ua stretched on kīβao frame), bifurcated rear leather tail (muþīta, Fig. 77; older īseso), girl's beaded katuŋgə & 700-cylinder 4.5-lb brass apron (kīmæŋgo of nžalī cylinders, Fig. 78), cotton ndamī, sandals (īatu) & sitting leather (kīdīþə); bead belt (kīβīna of blue kītætī), wire spirals (mulīa / ndīīa), cowries (ŋgūu), white neck-ring (īmīlī / īdatī), collar (ŋgala / īsoa) with Conus disc (kīβuo), iron-chain shoulder collar (ŋgunī, Fig. 79), metal beads (ndekə, Fig. 80), elder's ukumū (Figs. 81–82), fragrant kīulu & kīþua grass-root necklets (Fig. 83), Job's tears (ŋgaīə), metal armlets (kītaŋga / kīlaŋga, Figs. 84–87), upper-arm beadwork (īambə), elephant ivory/hind-foot armlets (ukoþo / ŋgoþo, Fig. 88), tin/copper ear-rings (īβulī, Figs. 89–92; ear-peg kīkulu) & finger-rings (ŋgomo); hairstyles (mutīundū, kīþuku), razor (wænzī), whetstone (īno), tweezers (ŋgosə & ŋgola, Figs. 93–95, riddle p. 389), kīutu perfume & pigments (mbu red ochre, ndo light red earth, īea white chalk, īkaŋga yellow Typha pollen); cicatrization (ndə īa kaβīu / mukuβa & ndækaþo, Figs. 96–102) & cheek tattooing (Fig. 103); and Teeth-Chipping (kuseuβīa maīo, Figs. 104–108: 6–8 teeth in Ulu, 4 in Kitui, 2 in E. Ukamba) with the ŋæsa chisel, hartebeest/goat-bone false teeth pegged into roots with an awl (mukuβa), removal of 2 lower incisors (kwīβa), and 2 dental riddles (p. 397). Ch. XXII Musical Instruments, Dances & Songs (pp. 398–417, Figs. 109–116): 4 drums—(1) mbalīa (ŋguþa in E. Ukamba, Fig. 109), (2) kīþæmbə (spirit/signal drum), (3) ŋgoma (double-headed), (4) kīa (formerly muβuŋgu, spool-shaped stamping drum with internal wire, Fig. 110); wire musical bow (uta, Fig. 111) & one-stringed bowed fiddle (mbæβə < Arabic rebab, Fig. 112, played by blind bard Kieti of Ikutha, p. 416); kudu/oryx horns, war-flute (ŋgulī), 4-hole bamboo flute (mutulælə), 1-m calabash-bell trumpet (so, pl. maso, Fig. 113), boys' reed clarionet (nžumalī, Fig. 114), and double Ricinus-stalk pipe (ŋgulī); dances (waþī, song-leader ŋguī who never eats lungs, dancing place kītuto): cheek-rubbing mbalīa (Figs. 115–116), afternoon musīa with leg bells (kīambə), maize-ripening kīlamu, Kitui stilt dance, married couples' īβūulu, and religious kīlumī, ŋgoma, mbæβo, kīesu & nžuma (with cow/zebra tail mwīŋgu). Ch. XXIII Toys & Games (īþau, kuþauka, pp. 418–428, Figs. 117–120): Crotalaria rattle (musīlī), stalk & clay dolls (Figs. 117–118), whip-top (nðælælī, Fig. 119), hoop (ndīa), calabash wheel (kīsæŋgæla kīa kīku), air-gun, plaited sling (kīkuþa, Fig. 120), bullroarer, hoop-spearing (kwaþa ndīa), 3 thought-reading games, river-crossing riddle game (mutumīa aβota / alīsa, p. 425), sand-hole guessing (kulīmana), hand-walking (kutambuka), frog-leap song ('kīoa wamandamanda...'), hide-and-seek (kwīβīþulanīla, cry 'kulu!'), 10-word counting-out rhyme ('tali, talita... mwitango', p. 426), Solanum marbles (kwaþa ndoŋgu), peg-jerking (kwaþa ŋgu), and kīūīśī (mancala board game with ŋgaīə seeds, pp. 427–428).",
    "part5EconomyAndPart6CharacteristicsAddendaChapters24To35": "Part V Economy (Chs. XXIV–XXXIII, pp. 429–545, Figs. 121–167): Ch. XXIV Village (Musīə, Figs. 121–124) & Hut (Nyumba, Fig. 125) (pp. 431–448): thorn fence (maþanzū), narrow entrance (muβīa), hanging-pole gate (Fig. 124), outside men's council tree (þomə, headed by mutumīa wa þomə), beehive hut with center post (kītuə), rafters (ŋgetī), partition (ututu), sleeping room (we), elastic stick bed (mwau, pl. mīau), wood store (kībæta), 13-part Hut Floor Plan (Fig. 125 p. 438), 20-second fire-drilling (male drill ulīndī + female hearth kīka, Fig. 126), hut-building rites ('wife's bow' = kīondo hung with husband's bow; eating ŋgīma before ritual coitus), refuse heap (utunda, pl. ndundə), raised storehouse (īkumbī / kītsumba), baby carrier (ŋgoī), and boys' spit challenge ('twīla mata, mβalaīnə!', p. 448). Chs. XXV–XXVI Weapons, Elephant Hunting & 7 Traps (pp. 449–474, Figs. 127–138): bow (uta: upper tip mbīa īa muþīa, lower tip mbīa īa ītīna, sinew string ua), arrows (musīə: notch mbalīo, wooden foreshaft uþunžī, iron head vīano, unfeathered shaft muluka, Fig. 127; weight 21.8 g / 19 g), arrow-poison (īβaī from muβaī Acocanthera + kīlīa mbitī, kalamba, kīþūī, spider mbua-mbuī, applied with spatula kīβalū, Fig. 128), bird arrows (īaŋgī, pl. maŋgī, with ndaī & nžæþo cross-pegs, Fig. 129), quiver (þīaka) & ostrich-plume kītuku (Fig. 130), sword (uβīu, sheath ndo, Fig. 131), dance stick (nžuma / ndata, Fig. 132), and leather-encased quartz-ball club (Fig. 133); elephant hunting party (nžīma / uīma < kusīma; novices forbidden to eat uβulīo or īþə or see uncut tail/trunk/dugs, pp. 465–469); and 7 traps (1. kīambu falling elephant spear, 2. īkæŋgə falling log Fig. 134, 3. live-hen pit noose, 4. mukwa & ndīndīlīo bent-pole running nooses Figs. 135–136, 5. īkæŋgə arched bird-snare Fig. 137, 6. stone-slab drop trap, 7. kītatī / kīsuŋgula hollow wooden mole trap Fig. 138, plus wild-fig bird-lime ulæmbwa / læmbwa p. 579). Chs. XXVII–XXXI Livestock, Beekeeping, Agriculture, Food & Stimulants (pp. 475–526, Figs. 139–155): 5 cattle origin myths, cattle-post (kīeŋgo), taming cattle with night-jar (īawə) ash (kuβoβīa), crook (mboloī), cow-bell (mbuī), castration (kuβakua vs. kutua), cattle-bleeding arrow (ndīa, Figs. 139–140), 28 Kamba livestock terms & 6 cow names (pp. 486–487), Malata wa Kyambi's beehive-passage rite for twin calves (pp. 489–490), and cattle diseases (ŋgaī, kīo kīa maβuī lung brand Fig. 141, muŋgutu / muþunī, īkænə, ndelu, nžamba leeches); beekeeping (mwatu, lid mbæŋgæo / mbalaa, hook mboloī, taming bees kuβoβīa with molo lizard & mutton, honey bag Fig. 142, container kīþæmbə, wax mawa, harvesting kutwa vs. wild-honey hunting kulaīa, telling bees of owner's death p. 499); agriculture (field munda, irrigation ditch mutau, clearing knife nžomo / kasomo Fig. 143, 2–3 m sod-breaking pole nde, woman's digging stick mīo / mwīo / kamolo Fig. 144, bird-scaring platform Fig. 145, threshing kuβūa on kīβuīo, granary kīīŋgə); food (blood-whisk muþekæþī Fig. 146, fresh milk īþumo, sour milk īīa, colostrum kīþana, milk gourd kītīa in kīkamī, butter mauta in kīþulī, dishes īsīo, usu eaten with index finger kīaa kīa kusuna, kīna, ŋgīma, kītækə, maize bread mukaə / mukatə / kīmutu / kīkīə, flying ants mba, resin kīþana); and stimulants (beer ukī wa kīwa / nžukī pounded with muþī in nde mortar, strained through kīkælə with kītumbu pulp, fermented with mwatīnə Kigelia, frothy top kīβuo, buying father's permission to drink beer kuþa / kuþaīa; tobacco kumbato & snuff mbakī + īatī natron in kīaŋgī bottles Figs. 147–152 & pipes ndozo Figs. 153–155). Chs. XXXII–XXXIII Industries & Division of Work (pp. 527–545, Figs. 156–167): iron (kīaa), ore (kīlea), tuyere (kīīa), charcoal (maka), smith (mutwī), hammer (kīβa, Fig. 156), tongs (mwīβīsto, Fig. 157; smith's word-taboos on ua, īumba, ŋgola p. 530); wire (uþuku) drawn through uta drawplate, wound on kīlīŋgī (Fig. 158), cut on kītatī (Fig. 161a) & pinched with ŋgola pincers (Fig. 161b–c) into triangular S-link chains (munīo, Fig. 159 vs. Kikuyu chain Fig. 160); axe (īþoka), adze (ŋgomo, Fig. 164), stools (kīβīla, woman's kītumbə Fig. 162, elder's mumbə Fig. 163); pottery (nyuŋgu, large kītænə, father's muna / kamuna, potter's marks Fig. 165); chewed-bark string (ulī, pl. ndīī, in kīlīŋga balls) plaited into kīondə & girl's dowry bag (nduŋgū, Figs. 166–167); and verbatim Rabai Kamba reading-book text (Kitabu tja kutsoma) on Woman's vs. Man's Work (pp. 543–545). Part VI Mental & Somatic Characteristics, Addenda, Bibliography, Illustrations, Errata & Index (Chs. XXXIV–XXXV, pp. 547–607): Dr. G. Backman's 70-subject anthropometric scheme, 60 Kitui prisoners' weight (mean 117.56 lbs, min 79.5 lbs, max 133 lbs), 6th finger/toe hexadactyly (child named ndula), pigeon-toed runners (ndaŋgī / matæŋgo), one-eyed/cross-eyed (nžoŋgo), blind (kīlīnda), 85-year-old woman who remembered Krapf (1849) & 5-generation elder Kituva at Machakos, pointing with the tongue (p. 565); Addenda (pp. 569–584): German East Africa diaspora (pp. 569–572), Chagga lineages from Ukamba (Useri, Mkuu, Keni, Mwika, Marangu [Chief Mareale from Umon/Mumoni, Ngovi], Oru, p. 573), Women's 7-word lexicon (p. 574), īþaβu wayside stone/branch-throwing custom & hiding fatal hunting arrow (pp. 576–577), 3 basic color roots (-īu black = soot mbīo, -æu / nžaū white = lime īea, -tunə red = clay ndo, color = īlaŋgī, p. 580), traditional women's market day (kīŋgaŋga, p. 580), Hamitic/Swahili loanwords (pp. 582–584), List of 60 Cited Works (pp. 585–589), List of 167 Figures (pp. 590–596), Errata (p. 597), and Index (pp. 599–607)."
  }
} as const;

export const STEERE_1870_COMPLETE_ETHNOGRAPHIC_SUMMARY = {
  "sourceId": "steere-swahili-tales-1870",
  "citation": "Steere, Edward, LL.D. (Rector of Little Steeping, Lincolnshire, and Chaplain to Bishop Tozer) (1870). Swahili Tales, as Told by Natives of Zanzibar. With an English Translation. London: Bell & Daldy, York Street, Covent Garden (Printed by William Clowes and Sons, Duke Street, Stamford Street, and Charing Cross), pp. i–xvi, 1–504 [PDF pp. 1–529].",
  "sections": {
    "frontMatterProvenanceAndPreface": "Front Matter, Provenance & Preface (pp. i–xvi, PDF pp. 1–20): Digitized from the personal presentation copy of Scottish folklorist John Francis Campbell of Islay (1821–1885, author of Popular Tales of the West Highlands; National Library of Scotland shelfmark Campbell.571; inscribed on flyleaf: 'J. F. Campbell, Augt. 1st 1870. Given to me by Mr. Ralston at the Brit: Museum, July 29th 1870. Read & noted. J. F. C.', with Campbell's marginal cross-references on p. vii and p. 422 matching 'Kisa cha Nyani, na Simba, na Nyoka' and 'Nussu-Bin-Nussu' to the Arabic Kalila and Dimna [Knatchbull's translation, Oxford 1819, pp. 340, 81]). Preface by Edward Steere (pp. v–xiv): names all 6 primary informants—(1) Hamisi wa Kayi (Khamis bin Abubekr, of Mbweni on the Mrima opposite Zanzibar, who dictated 'Kisa cha Punda wa Dobi', 'Sultani Darai', 'Kisa cha Mwanamke na Mtoto wa Nzige', 'Mifano' [pp. 191–195], 'Hadithi ya Mtoto wa Sultani na kijana wake Goso' [which Steere remarks on p. viii was evidently never anything but pure African], 'Goso, Mwalimu wa Vijana', '\"Uza Ghali, Si Uza Rakhisi\"', 'Hadithi ya Hasseebu Kareem ed Deen na Sultani wa Nyoka', 'Vitendawili' [pp. 417–421], and 'Hadithi ya Liongo' [pp. 439–453]), (2) Masazo (narrator of 'Mfalme na Wanawe saba', 'Sultani Majinuni' [the master-piece of complexity], 'Kisa cha Kuku na Mwewe', and 'Ao Rathi, Ao Mali', closing tales with 'Ikiwa njema, njema yetu wote, na ikiwa mbaya, mbaya yangu mimi mosi niliyoifanya'), (3) Ali bin Moallim (half-caste Arab, narrator of 'Nussu-Bin-Nussu' and 'Hadithi ya Mohammed Mtepetevu'), (4) Mohammed bin Abd en Nuri (Kathi and grandson of Sheikh Muhyiddin, former chief judge of Mombasa and Zanzibar, narrator of the Kimvita tale 'Kititi, na Fisi, na Simba', pp. 325–329, and composer of the 12-line religious poem on p. xiii), (5) Munyi Khatibu of Mtang'ata on the mainland opposite Pemba (narrator of 'Kisa cha Nyani, na Simba, na Nyoka' and 'Simba na Kulungu', pp. 423–437), and (6) Hassan bin Yusuf (transcriber and explainer of 'Mashairi ya Liongo' [composed in 5-line takhmisi stanzas by Sheikh Abdallah bin Ali bin Nasir, called Muyuweni, of Mombasa, incorporating Liongo Fumo's ancient Kingozi couplets], 'Utumbuizo wa Gungu' ['Gungu la Kufunda' & 'Gungu la Kukwaa'], and 'Mwanzo wa Utenzi wa Ayubu', pp. 455–487); plus 3 Mombasa poems in the Preface (pp. xii–xiii: the satirical verse against the Wakilindini 'Wakilindini si watu, ni punda wa milia... Wa kwanza ni Shahaifu, wa pili ni Bwana Ngia', the floral quatrain on the yellow acacia blossom 'Kijiti chema cha mnga / Chaota kumbi na kumbi, / Mtu mjinga mjinga / Aweza kutinda jombi', and Mohammed bin Abd en Nuri's prayer poem).",
    "contentsAndTales1To6": "Contents (pp. xv–xvi) & Tales 1–6 (pp. 1–283): (1) Kisa cha Punda wa Dobi (An Ass's Heart / The Washerman's Donkey, pp. 1–11: monkey tricked onto shark's back claims he left his heart and ears hanging in a mkuyu tree); (2) Sultani Darai (pp. 13–137: maiden raised after her mother dies eating a ngurukia fish, abandoned by her father via a single grain of mtama, aided by the gazelle Paa who slays the seven-headed dragon Nunda / joka la mji wa Chini with the sword Upanga wa Wino and receives a magnificent funeral with mkeka and sanda on p. 114, while a faithful old nurse carries her to a crystal palace when the stepmother sends poisoned vyungu); (3) Kisa cha Mwanamke na Mtoto wa Nzige (An Indian Story of a Woman and the Grasshopper's Child, pp. 139–147: cumulative Hindi/Banyan chant 'Aré baya tené maré...'); (4) Mfalme na Wanawe saba (Two Kings and Their Seven Sons, pp. 149–189: a silence contest broken by a golden statue concealing a youth); (5) Mifano (Proverbs #1–#20, pp. 191–195); and (6) Sultani Majinuni (pp. 197–283: giant Nunda-bird steals the dates of the garden of Ajemi until caught at the third cock-crow by seventh son Sitahabu playing bao with coffee and tambuu; Nunda-mla-watu grows from a greedy cat through 9 escalating prey tiers—chickens, goats, cattle, donkeys, horses, camels, elephants, soldiers—before being slain at sunrise with spear and musket by seventh slave-son Hadhara; includes the 'Chini ya mbingu na juu ya nti!' sky-oath and the naming of 8 coastal delicacies on pp. 262, 274–276).",
    "tales7To17AndVitendawili": "Tales 7–17 & Vitendawili (pp. 285–453): (7) Goso, Mwalimu wa Vijana (Goso, the Teacher, pp. 285–291: cumulative chain-rhyme after a mabuyu fruit falls from a mbuyu baobab and breaks his neck: 'Na koonde i wapi? Koonde imeliwa na ng'ombe... Na mchinjaji yu wapi? Amekufa.'); (8) \"Uza Ghali, Si Uza Rakhisi\" (\"Sell Dear, Not Cheap\", pp. 293–323: Ali's 4 symbolic riddle-cargoes solved by Vizier Shamsi's daughter after 40-day truces: two pearls = life vs. death, two identical mares = mother vs. filly tested with grass vs. chaff, two ebony staffs = top vs. root tested in water, and the three maxims); (9) Kititi, na Fisi, na Simba (The Hare, the Hyæna, and the Lion, in Kimvita [Mombasa Dialect], pp. 325–329: Kititi [= Kiunguja Sungura] deceives Fisi and traps Simba in a roof-thatching makuti pinch with two philosophical riddles on earth/sky and eyes/shame); (10) Hadithi ya Hasseebu Kareem ed Deen na Sultani wa Nyoka (The Story of Hasseebu Kareem ed Deen and the King of the Snakes, pp. 331–361: jamdani ya nguo, Bulukiya, Jamaan, and the serpent queen); (11) Kisa cha Kuku na Mwewe (The Hen and the Hawk, pp. 363–365: why the hen always scratches the ground looking for the hawk's lost razor wembe); (12) Hadithi ya Mtoto wa Sultani na kijana wake Goso (Blessing or Property / The Sultan's Son and His Lad Goso, pp. 367–377: pure African tale with kipeo, zimwi, and the ring pete that summons clothes and stone mansions); (13) Hadithi ya Mohammed Mtepetevu (The Story of Mohammed the Languid, pp. 379–389: Medinat es Salaam, Abu l-Muzaffar, and the ape/ifrit); (14) Ao Rathi, Ao Mali (Blessing or Property, pp. 391–415: younger son chooses parents' blessing radhi over wealth mali, saves four animals [panya, kobe, nyoka, mwewe] who repeat 'Niponye la jua, nitakuponya la mvua', and receives the snake king's magic ring); (15) Vitendawili (Enigmas #1–#13 on pp. 417–421 + Enigma #14 on p. 492 Note 6, opened with 'Kitendawili! — Tega!' and closed with the prize-town formula 'Nipe mji! — Nimekupa Unguja / Mvita / Kilwa...'); (16) Kisa cha Nyani, na Simba, na Nyoka (The Ape, the Lion, and the Snake, pp. 423–431: Munyi Khatibu's Mtang'ata tale of Kiroboto the Woodcutter [mchanja kuni], the grateful animals, and human ingratitude 'Mwanadamu hana fadhili'); (17) Simba na Kulungu (The Lion and the Bushbuck, pp. 433–437: the rabbit Sungura leads the huntsman into the pit with the proverb 'Kucheka na kununa ni ipi ilio njema?'); and (18) Hadithi ya Liongo (The Story of Liongo Fumo of Shanga/Shaka near the Ozi River, pp. 439–453: prison song to slave-girl Kijakazi Saa pp. 442–443 encoding the file tupa and bran chaff chachu files, escape by crashing fetters pingu and cadenas mnyororo, the kikoa coconut-climbing plot on pp. 444–447, and betrayal by his nephew using a copper needle sindano ya shaba in the navel kitovu near the well before dying kneeling with bow and arrow for three days).",
    "liongoGunguAyubuPoetryNotesAndCorrigenda": "Classical Kingozi/Kiamu Poetry, 37 Italicized Words, 103 Notes & 12 Corrigenda (pp. 455–504): (19) Mashairi ya Liongo (29五-line takhmisi stanzas on pp. 455–469 + 7-stanza Epilogue on pp. 470–471 with parallel Kingozi/Kiamu, Kiunguja, and English columns); (20) Utumbuizo wa Gungu (Dance Song of the Gungu: 'Gungu la Kufunda' [20 stanzas, pp. 474–479] & 'Gungu la Kukwaa' [15 couplets + chorus, pp. 478–483] with triple Kingozi/Kiunguja/English columns); (21) Mwanzo wa Utenzi wa Ayubu (The Beginning of the Poem of Job: 16 four-line utenzi stanzas, pp. 485–487); (22) Explanations of Words in Italics (p. 490: all 37 untranslated terms from Askari to Zambarau); (23) Notes #1–#103 (pp. 490–503: complete ethnographic, culinary, architectural, legal, maritime, and grammatical commentary); and (24) Corrigenda (p. 504: all 12 authorial corrections across pp. 2, 18, 22, 61, 134, 152, 193 No. 8, 263, 290, 295, 299, 444)."
  }
} as const;

export const STEERE_MADAN_1894_COMPLETE_ETHNOGRAPHIC_SUMMARY = {
  "sourceId": "steere-madan-swahili-handbook-1894",
  "citation": "Steere, Edward, LL.D. (Missionary Bishop for Central Africa) & Madan, A. C., M.A. (Senior Student and Formerly Tutor of Ch. Ch. Oxford) (1894). A Handbook of the Swahili Language, as Spoken at Zanzibar. Edited for the Universities' Mission to Central Africa by the Late Edward Steere, LL.D. Fourth Edition, Revised and Enlarged by A. C. Madan, M.A. London: Society for Promoting Christian Knowledge (SPCK), Northumberland Avenue, W.C.; 43, Queen Victoria Street, E.C.; Brighton: 135, North Street (Printed by William Clowes and Sons, Limited, London and Beccles), pp. i–xxii, 1–458 [PDF pp. 1–488].",
  "sections": {
    "frontMatterProvenanceAdvertisementsAndPreface": "Front Matter, Provenance, Advertisements to 2nd & 3rd Editions, Table of Contents & Preface to 1st Edition (pp. i–xxii, PDF pp. 1–23): Digitized from the University of Toronto Library copy (Barcode 3 1761 00101630 2, Call No. PL 8702 S724 1894, stamped JAN 9 1970, microformed AUG - 9 1989; Internet Archive http://www.archive.org/details/handbookofswahil00steeuoft), featuring the pencil title-page inscription 'CHUO CHA KISWAHILI', 3 chromolithographed color floral plates (PDF pp. 10, 262, 448), and rich reader marginalia throughout (including p. 6 Arabic ligature امغزف for the Kilwa letter amekufa/amevuka anecdote, p. 429 5-line cursive Swahili note 'Bwana wangu ninyemheshimu sana...', and p. 453 vocalized Ajami transcription of Phrase 197 سَلَامُ سَانَ حَجَ يَاغُ يَكُعِشِ وِوِ سَانَ نَفُرَاحَ). Advertisement to the Second Edition (p. xv, London, January, 1875, signed Edward Steere): explains omitting the old Second Class of Substantives because all animate nouns take Class I concords. Advertisement to the Third Edition (pp. xvii–xix, Zanzibar, Christmas, 1882, signed A. C. M. [A. C. Madan]): records that Bishop Steere spent some of the last hours of his life revising the Handbook, incorporates words from his notes into Part II, adds Appendix V with Ven. Archdeacon Hodgson's approval, and notes in the P.S. that Dr. Krapf's printed 1882 Dictionary reached the Bishop too late for use. Preface to the First Edition (pp. iii–xiii, Little Steeping, May, 1870, signed Edward Steere): arrival in Zanzibar with Bishop Tozer at the end of August 1864; copies of vocabularies by Dr. Krapf, Rev. J. Rebmann (copied at Mombas Nov. 1864), Mr. Witt & Mr. Schultz (of O'Swald & Co.), Baron von der Decken & Dr. Kersten, and Rev. Thomas Wakefield; printing begun July 1865; primary informants Hamis wa Tani and his son Mohammed bin Khamis (who revised the Substantives, Adjectives, Pronouns, and Verbs on Saturday mornings before sailing for Bombay), Hamis wa Kayi (Hadithi za Kiunguja and half of Krapf's MS dictionary brought by Rev. R. L. Pennell), Sheikh 'Abd al 'Aziz (classical Swahili translation of Psalms 1–16), Sheikh Mohammed bin Ali & Hassan bin Yusuf (classical poetry), Rev. G. E. Drayton & Mrs. Drayton (died Nov. 1867), Rev. W. & Mrs. Lea (Psalm 119 & Part II arrangement), and critique of Père Etienne Baur's French-Swahili Catechism; plus collections in Shambala (1866–1867, for Rev. C. A. Alington), Yao/Achowa, Nyamwezi, Gindo, Zaramo, and Ngazidja, and the jesting native etymology of Swahili from Sawa hila ('All same cheat', p. xiii) vs. Arabic Sawáhil (pl. of Sahil, coast).",
    "part1IntroductionAlphabetSubstantivesAndAdjectives": "Part I — Introduction, Alphabet, Substantives & Adjectives/Numerals (Book pp. 1–101, PDF pp. 24–124): Introduction (pp. 1–7): Bleek's Zangian genus; critique of Frédoux's Sechuana class numbering; rules of broken 'foreigners' Swahili' (forming all plurals with ma- or nyingi, conjugating verbs solely with na-/ma-/ta-, and negating with hakuna, pp. 3–4); and the Kilwa letter ambiguity (امغزف read as amekufa 'had died' vs. amevuka 'had got away', p. 6). The Alphabet (pp. 8–15): 5 Italian vowels, consonants, Arabic gh/kh/th (italic th = voiceless as in think, roman th = voiced as in they), ng', ny, explosive aspirated p', t', k' (from suppressed n, e.g. upepo → p'epo, upanga → p'anga, ukuta → k'uta, p. 11), latent l between vowels (Merima kufala = Zanzibar kufaa; mzambarau → Mzambaraúni, p. 13), penultimate accent shifted by -ni, and syllable division proven by Kinyume. Substantives (pp. 16–82): 8 Noun Classes (Classes I–VIII), 3 locative concords of -ni (mw- inside, p- near, kw- to/at, pp. 21–22), and categorized English–Swahili lexicon (pp. 24–82) including 9 Antelopes (p. 25: Bara, Dondoro, Koru, Kuguni, Mpofu, Nyumbo, Parahara, Kulungu, Paa), 14 Baskets & Matting Bags (pp. 26–27: Kikapu/Chikapu, Kapu, Kanda, Junia/Gunia, Kigunni, Kifumbo, Pakacha, Dohani, Tunga, Ungo/Uteo, Kiteo, Kunguto, Jamanda), 6 Cakes (p. 30: Mkate wa mofu, Mkate wa tumbako, Bumunda, Kitumbua, Ladu, Kinyunya), 26 Coconut terms (pp. 33–34: Mnazi, kichilema/shaha, kilifu, kuti, uchukuti, ukuti, makuti ya kumba/pande/viungo, pakacha, karara, upunga, kidaka, kitale, dafu, koroma, nazi, joya, mbata, makumbi, usumba, kifuo, kifuu, mbuzi ya kukunia nazi, tui, kifumbu, chicha, nazi kavu, mafuta ya nazi), 8 Dhow types & parts (p. 38: Dau, Mtepe, Betela, Bágala [mlingote wa kalmi], Ghangi, Batili, Bedeni, Awesia), 5 Dollar coins (p. 39: reale ya mizinga, reale ya Sham, reale ya thahabu, reale ya kifransa/bwara), Men's & Women's Dress (p. 40), Fish (p. 43), 6 Games (p. 45: Bao, Maliyandimu, Tiabu, Dama, Tasa, Tinge), 3 Handfuls (p. 47: ukufi, chopa, konzi), Dr. Krapf's Delay Proverb (p. 48: 'Kesho na kesho ni madanganya, ao ni makatazanya. — Si madanganya wala si makatazanya, ni mazuizanya.'), 11 Musical Instruments (p. 57), 10 Rice Varieties (p. 65: Mpunga, Mchele, Wali, Ukoko, Masalufu,Bungala, Kapwai, Madevu,uchukwi, Kimoto, Sifara,Kichooko, Shindano, Sena,Bora kupata), Seasons (p. 68: Kaskazi, Maleleji, Masika, Mchoo, Vuli, Kipupwe, Kusi, Demani), 3 Shamba Servants (p. 68: Msimamizi, Nokoa, Kadamu), 7 Evil Spirits (p. 71: Pepo, Shetani, Jini, Mahoka, Kinyamkela, Dungumaro, Koikoi, Mwana maua), Divisions of Time (pp. 75–77: 12 Arab/Swahili lunar months Mfunguo wa mosi to Ramathani, 365-day Solar/Nautical year from Siku a mwaka / Kigunzi reckoned in 10-day miongo decades from Mwongo mosi to Mwongo miamoja wa sitini, daakuu 2:30 A.M. Ramathan gun, 7 weekdays, and 18 daily/prayer time points including Mafungulia ng'ombe makuu [8 A.M.] and madogo [9 A.M.]), Weights (pp. 80–81: Wakia, Rattel, Mani, Pishi, Frasila, Mzo, Farra ya mti = 72 rattel or 12 pishi), 8 Woods (p. 82), and Wahadimu & Munyi mkuu of Zanzibar (p. 82). Adjectives & Numerals (pp. 83–101): Concord rules, 5 ways to supply missing adjectives, Comparison (kuliko, zayidi ya, punde, kupita, kuzidi/kupungua), Swahili & Arabic Numerals 1–1,000,000 (mosi–kumi, fungate, mwongo, kikwi, lakki, milyón), 8-Class Numeral Table (pp. 91–92), Ordinals, 16 Fractions of a Dollar (p. 93: Nuss ya themuni 1/16, Themuni/Ze'mni 1/8, Zerenge 1/5, Robo 1/4, Nuss 1/2, Kassa robo 3/4, Kassa themuni 7/8, Kassa nuss ya themuni 15/16), and List of Adjectives (pp. 94–101, including -nyangálika p. 94).",
    "part1PronounsVerbsAdverbsAndComparativeTables": "Part I — Pronouns, Verbs, Adverbs, Prepositions, Conjunctions, Interjections/Salutations, Word-Formation & 9-Language Comparative Tables (Book pp. 102–235, PDF pp. 125–258): Pronouns (pp. 102–124): Personal pronouns & Na- contractions (nami–nao), Subject/Object prefixes & poetic enclitic object suffixes (p. 108: hukunazo = hukuwa nazo, umenifanyiziaje), Possessives & enclitics (pp. 109–112: mwanangu, mumewe, mkeo, jinalo, shogaye, nyumbani mwake/pake/kwake), Reflectives (-ji-, nafsi, moyo, -enyewe, peke), 3 Demonstrative series (huyu/huyo/yule) + Copular ndimi–ndiko & negative siye–sicho (pp. 113–117), Relatives (-ye-/-o- with -na-/-li-/-taka-, tenseless suffixal aliyempenda → ampendaye, negative asiyependa, and manner/time/place -vyo, -po, -ko, -mo, pp. 117–122), and Interrogatives (nani, nini/-ni, lini, gani, -pi, wapi, -je, -ngapi, pp. 122–124). Verbs (pp. 125–208): Indefinite (hu- & tenseless relative), Present (-a-, -na-), Present Perfect (-me-), Past Perfect (-li-/-ali- & continuous -li-ki-), Narrative Past (-ka- & nika- → ha-), Future (-ta-/-taka-), 4 Conditionals (-ki- [with niki- → hi-], -japo-, -nge-/-nga-, -ngali-), Imperative, Subjunctive, Infinitive (including A. C. Madan's bracketed note on p. 142 on preposed intensive infinitives kufa utakufa and coordinated infinitives anayeabudiwa na kutukuzwa), Negative Conjugation (si-/ha-...-i, -ku-, -ja- [with kabla/bado], -ta-, -sipo-, -nge-, -ngali-, -si-...-e, -sije-, kutoa/kuto-), Passive (-w-, -liwa/-lewa), Auxiliaries (kuwa, kutoa, kuja, kwisha, kupasa), 4 Derivative Forms (Applied -ia/-ea/-ilia/-elea, Causative -isha/-esha/-iza/-eza/-za/-ya, Neuter/Quasi-passive -ka, Reciprocal -ana & -kana) + Reduplication, and List of Verbs (pp. 165–208). Adverbs, Prepositions, Conjunctions, Interjections & Salutations (pp. 209–225): Sikamoo (< Nashika mguu wako) — Marahaba; Jambo / Jambo sana — Jambo sana, kama lulu / kama marijani; Hujambo? — Sijambo; U hali gani? — Njema / Nzuri; Subalkheri, Masalkheri, Kwa heri — Kwa heri ya kuonana; and 24 Interjections (Ah! to Weye!). Formation of Words (pp. 226–233): Arabic, Portuguese (mvinyo, gereza, bendera, meza, leso, bweta, sapatu), French & English loans, reversive -ua, causative -ya, de-verbal adjectives in -vu/-fu, agent nouns in m-...-i/-ji/-si/-shi/-zi, result/place nouns in -o, and abstracts in u-. Comparative Tables (pp. 234–235): Noun Prefixes across 9 Bantu Languages (Swahili, Shambala, [Ki]Nyika, [Ki]Kamba, Nyamwezi, Yao, Kafir, [Se]Chuana, [Otyi]Herero, p. 234) and Preposition -a across 4 Languages (Swahili, Shambala, Nyamwezi, Yao, p. 235).",
    "part2PreliminaryObservationsAndSwahiliEnglishVocabulary": "Part II — Preliminary Observations & Swahili–English Vocabulary (Book pp. 237–421, PDF pp. 263–447): Preliminary Observations (pp. 239–243): 12 rules for stripping terminations/enclitics (-ni, -pi, -je, -po/-ko/-mo, -ye/-o, -o, -ngu/-ko/-ke/-tu/-nu/-o, -ka, -na, -wa, -ia/-ea/-liwa/-lewa, -isha/-esha/-iza/-eza), Tables of Substantival (17), Adjectival (18), Pronominal (18), and Verbal Prefixes (47), and Dialect Sound-Correspondence Table (p. 243: Zanzibar ch = t [M.], e = a [M.], h = kh [Ar.], j = d [M.] / y [A., N.], t = ch [Ozi.], v = z [A.], z = v [Mer.] / th [Patta and Ozi.] / j [vulgar]). Swahili–English Vocabulary (pp. 245–421, ~5,500 headwords from A to Zuzuka ku-): includes the 18 Named Parts of a Man's Kanzu (s.v. Kanzu, p. 299: Tao la kanzu, Magongo nene, Badani [Kimo], Taharizi, Sijafu, Vikwapa, Lisani, Jabali, Mhalbori, Kaaba, Shada, Kitanzi, Kazi ya shingo [Tiki, Mrera, Viboko, Vinara], Darizi, Mjuzi [Ar. shararaji], Mkia wa mjuzi, Vipaji [viguu], Kanzu ya ziki); all 41 Named Mita (Quarters/Districts) of Zanzibar Town (s.v. Mta, pp. 346–347: 30 on the Peninsula—Shangani, Baghani, Gerezani, Forthani, Mita ya pwani, Kiponda, Mbuyuni, Malindi, Funguni, Jungiani, Kokoni, Mkunazini, Kibokoni, Kidutani, Mzambarauni, Kijukakuni, Vuga, Mnazimoja, Mji mpya, Mtakuja, Jumea, Soko la Mahogo, Kajificheni, Mfuuni, Migomboni, Tiuyani, Sham, Hurumzi, Kutani, Mwavi—and 11 across the Creek at Ng'ambo—Ng'ambo, Mchangani, Gulioni, Kwa Buki, Vikokotoni, Kisimani kwa kema, Mchinjani, Mwembe mjugu, Kikwajuni, Mkadini, Mnazi moja); Muhadimu $2 house-tax shared between Seyed Majid and the Munyi mkuu at Dunga (s.v. Muhadimu, p. 349); and embedded proverbs, bird calls & cultural verses: Mvundánti, mwanánti (s.v. -a, p. 268), Chipukizi ndio mti (p. 262), Hua dove cries ('Mama akafa, Baba akafa, nimesalia mimi, tu, tu, tu, tu, tu' & 'Kuku mfupa mtupu, mimi nyama tele tele tele', p. 287), Kazi mbi si mtezo mwema? (p. 301), Kiroboto Hathramaut marching parody ('Kiroboto, Kiroboto, tia moto, tia moto', p. 311), Akupaye kishogo si mwenzio (p. 312), Kurumbizi bird cry ('Mtu anasema na mume', p. 322), Fathili ya punda ni mashuzi (p. 333), Uzuri wa mkasasi, / Ukipata, maji, basi (p. 341), and Mwigo omen-bird dialogue ('Kooo! — Mwigo! — Kooo! — Niagulie! — Kooo! — Kwema nendako? — Kooo!', p. 352).",
    "part2Appendices1To5": "Part II — Appendices I–V (Book pp. 423–458, PDF pp. 449–484): Appendix I — Specimens of Kinyume (pp. 425–426): all 52 verbatim syllable-inversion pairs written out by Johari, a native of the Swahili coast a little south of Zanzibar, in the Merima dialect (from Ng'ombe → Mbeng'o to Msahafu → Fumsaha, including Mguru → Rumgu 'A leg', Mwanamuke → Kemwanamu 'A woman', Njara inaniuma → Ranja mainaniu 'I ache with hunger', Twende zetu → Tutwendeze 'Let us be off', Senendi → Ndisene 'I am not going', Mutu → Tumu 'Man'). Appendix II — Specimens of Swahili Correspondence (pp. 427–429): 2 Prose Letters (Letter 1 to D. S., il Mwingrezi from H. bin A. sending 7 fowls, and Letter 2 from M. wadi S. dated 26 mwezi wa mfunguo kenda asking pardon) + 2 Poetic Epistles (16-line poem 'Risala enda kwa hima / Kwa Edward Istira...' and the Pemba–Mombasa quatrain 'Hatukiati kisiwa kwa uvambume na upembe...') + note on Kingozi -ile/-ene perfects (mbwene = nimeona, nikomile = nimekoma) and p. 429 reader cursive note ('Bwana wangu ninyemheshimu sana...'). Appendix III — Part of a Swahili Tale with Literal & Free Translation (pp. 431–442): word-for-word morphological parsing of 'Hadithi ya Waridi, na Ureda, na Jindi, na Sinébar' (opening 'Aliondokea mtu tajiri', Sinébar lowered into the well with the 7-headed snake nyoka wa vichwa saba, the magic invisibility cap kofia mbovu, and the magic iron lamp taa mbovumbovu ya chuma that summons two men with gold or two men with cudgels vigongo). Appendix IV — Useful and Idiomatic Phrases (pp. 443–453): all 197 numbered conversational phrases (Nos. 1–54 collected with Mrs. G. E. Drayton; Nos. 55–197 arranged alphabetically, closing with #195 Kuamba na kusengenya, #196 asking a name, and #197 leave-taking 'Unayo haja tena? — Haja yangu ya kuishi wewe sana na furaha' with p. 453 reader Ajami transcription). Appendix V — On Money, Weights, and Measures in Zanzibar (pp. 455–458, compiled from Bishop Steere's notices and tables furnished to Rev. P. L. Jones-Bateman by a Mission servant): (1) Money (pesa, robo pesa [3 = 1 pesa], rupia = 60–64 pice = 1s. 8d., reale = 2 rupees 8 pice = 3s. 7d., English sovereign = 12 rupees, and legal tender scale 47 dollars = 100 rupees, 94 dollars = 200 rupees, 141 dollars = 300 rupees); (2) Measures of Length (wanda, shibiri kamili & morita, thiraa kamili & thiraa konde, wari, pima, uayo, futi; Dunga = saa 'nne [4 hrs], Kokotoni = saa sita [6 hrs]); (3) Measures of Weight (16 wakia = 1 rátel; 3 rátel = 1 mani; 6 rátel = 1 pishi; 6 pishi = 1 frasila [~35 lbs.]; 10 frasila = 1 mzo; 64 frasila = 1 ton); (4) Measures of Capacity (kibaba cha tele vs. cha mfuto; 2 vibaba = 1 kisaga; 2 visaga = 1 pishi; gunia & kanda regional pishi tables for Kwale/Mgao/Mvita vs. Pangani/Tanga/Mtang'ata; korja = 20 poles, boma, tanuu/kikapo, jura = 35 yds calico); and (5) The 32-Point Compass (p. 458: Jaa, Kutubu, Matlai, Magaribi, and all 14 eastern/western star-bearing pairs from Faragadi, Nash, Nagr, Luagr, Dayabu, Semak, Seria to Sosa, Tiri, Lakadiri, Lakarabu, Hamareni, Seheli, and Sonoobari)."
  }
} as const;

